A||||5||cmc||Questions from a Fiero project car owner-to-be||||1||
Z||000000||cmc||02-24-2001||05:05 PM||||So I'm going to start the adventure that is owning one of these fine four-cylinder Fieros in a month or so (shortly <I>before</I> I'm old enough to drive, but right after I get enough money).  I have my eye on a very well-priced '88 Fiero 2m4, 97K miles, been practically sitting at the lot for about 2 years (moved periodically to be washed).  Runs fine, makes the four-cylinder sounds, all the VIN plaques match, etc.<P>I do have a couple questions, though.<P>First of all, I've noticed in all the pictures I've seen in the Fiero picture gallery have a tach where '0' is off to the right a little; this one (an automatic) starts off where just about every other tach starts--to the left.  Looks very stock.  Is this actually a custom thing, or was it something they only put on base models, or automatics, or what?<P>Secondly, I do plan on doing an engine and transmission swap.  95 HP isn't a whole lot no matter how small the car is.  I'm mostly interested in a Quad-4 swap.  Given a freer-flowing exhaust, what have you Quad-4 HO owners get in terms of horsepower?  Also, will the engine be as the hornet, loud and buzzy?  (Those of you who have raced people my age that own Civics know what I'm talking about.)<P>I've heard that the Quad-4 is a good swap because it starts at 3000 and keeps on going up to the 6000s, rather than peaking at a rather low speed like the 3.8L engine some have swapped into it.  However, I've also heard that it allows for better weight distribution because it's lighter than most other engines; is this true, and to what extent?  Are there any better engines to swap into this car for this type of performance?<P>Finally: Hi, I'm Chris.<p>[This message has been edited by cmc (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||02-24-2001||06:59 PM||||Welcome to the forum Chris. The tach you have described sounds normal for the 4 cyl, the V6 tach is the one that starts lower. <BR>I don't know much about the quad 4. I like to have at least 6 cylinders. I had the 4 cyl. in mine and I swapped in the 2.8 Go for a 3800 if you really want power.<P>Good luck and keep us posted.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000002||FieroJoe||02-24-2001||07:01 PM||||I can't help you out on the swap info, but I can with the tach.<P>First off, does the car actually say 2m4 on the decklid?  If it does, it isn't a 88, or they have the wrong sticker on there.  You can tell if it is an 88 by looking to see if there are two holes for muffler tips, but only the left one will have the exhaust tip in it.<P>About the tach:  Fieros came with 1 of 2 different gauge clusters, not including the aux gauge setup.  4 cyl fieros and I believe 85 6 cyl had the 85mph cluster.  The tach on those is as you described it.  With most V6's, they had a 120mph speedo, and the tach had a higher redline in it, and also was jacked down to the left as you have described it.  120 mph clusters were also backlit, while 85mph clusters had "reflection" lighting.<P>by the way, Welcome to the Forum!||||reg||1
Z||000003||cmc||02-24-2001||07:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>I can't help you out on the swap info, but I can with the tach.<P>First off, does the car actually say 2m4 on the decklid?  If it does, it isn't a 88, or they have the wrong sticker on there.  You can tell if it is an 88 by looking to see if there are two holes for muffler tips, but only the left one will have the exhaust tip in it.<P>About the tach:  Fieros came with 1 of 2 different gauge clusters, not including the aux gauge setup.  4 cyl fieros and I believe 85 6 cyl had the 85mph cluster.  The tach on those is as you described it.  With most V6's, they had a 120mph speedo, and the tach had a higher redline in it, and also was jacked down to the left as you have described it.  120 mph clusters were also backlit, while 85mph clusters had "reflection" lighting.<P>by the way, Welcome to the Forum!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks.<P>On the 2m4 thing; no, I actually thought all years had them and this one was removed.<P>Yes, the lower rear fascia was cut for two outlets, but a single outlet exhaust system was installed (I checked under the car because I was worried that one side had possibly been rusted or broken off).  I haven't had a chance to see the full interior lighting, but what I have noticed about the interior is that it is in decent condition, albeit not in as good condition as the outside.  The seats have a few stains of what look like fast food--no cigarette burns anywhere.  Headliner is in good condition, but there's a broken speaker grille on the upper right side.<P>Further verifying it being an '88, I checked the VIN number with fiero.net's VIN decoder, and it is indeed what it claims to be: A four cylinder, notchback, '88 Fiero (1G2PE11R4JP2, for what it's worth I memorized that part the last time I went to look at it).||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||02-24-2001||07:41 PM||||&gt;rather than peaking at a rather low speed like the 3.8L engine some have swapped into it. <P>Welcome to the forum.<P>The 3800SC will smoke the quad4   All things being equal (which they never are)<P>Thinking of engine swapping wo ever owning/working on a car?  <P>  Well, okay.  I like ambition.  Go for it, but be prepared ...||||reg||1
Z||000005||cmc||02-24-2001||08:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gabriel:<BR><B>&gt;rather than peaking at a rather low speed like the 3.8L engine some have swapped into it. <P>Welcome to the forum.<P>The 3800SC will smoke the quad4   All things being equal (which they never are)<P>Thinking of engine swapping wo ever owning/working on a car?  <P>  Well, okay.  I like ambition.  Go for it, but be prepared ...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've worked on cars before, but I can't say I've taken an engine out on my own (or done much of the work involved).  Granted, I've never actually worked on a transverse-mounted engine before either--that will be one of the bigger challenges.  But I think I can do it with enough information from books/the Internet and help from my brother (more experienced in this sort of thing).<P>As for having transportation, there's a Bronco II (I hastily admit that I am a blue-oval owner, technically speaking) that needs a carburetor and a paint job that was given to me by my brother.  Decent parts hauler, too.<P>My biggest question on bigger engines vs. smaller engines is: How "not neutral" are other engine swaps in cars in handling when compared to Duke and Quad-4 cars?  What manual transaxle can most easily fit the 3.8 and its torque?  (Okay, that was two.)||||reg||1
